IT_GLOSS_GIT_TIMEOUT`. The message does name it.\n   - **Leftover state:** a kill between `git notes add` and the push leaves `PUSH_REF` and `REVIEWS_PUSH_REF` behind. The next sync does clean them up (`update-ref -d`), so this is small.\n   - **Fix:** use a longer or no limit for network subcommands.\n\n2. **Minor — `src/commands/sync.ts:152,190`, and the `gitTry` cleanups in `addReviews` and `sync` — read.** The author's own TODO already lists the `finally` case (a timeout in cleanup replaces the original error). The same pattern appears in the push catch blocks.\n   - **Scenario:** a `git push` times out and the error propagates correctly. A later cleanup `gitTry` in a `finally` also times out, and that second error masks the first.\n   - **Result:** the user sees a misleading error, and temporary refs can be left behind until the next sync.\n\nEverything else I read looks right.\n- The `rethrowTimeout` call sites cover the places that turn a git failure into a value (`carry`, `show`, `config`, `versions`, `reviews`, `sync`).\n- The remaining catch blocks only handle JSON parse errors.\n- `rawNote` rethrows anything that isn't \"no note found\", so a timeout propagates.\n- The `timeout`-wrapper logic in `test/run.ts` looks consistent.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
