ence covers only some of the sites.** The cited evidence is \"carry and config tests\". Those cover `targetState`, the carry source read, `trustedRef` and `configAt`. Five added `rethrowTimeout` calls have no test: `show.ts` missing-groups catch (h6), `createdAt` (h8), `versions.ts` `summary` (h16), `reviews.ts` `stamp` (h24), and the `gitTry` rethrow in h22. A regression that re-swallows a timeout at any of these would leave the suite green, which is the exact bug class this group claims to fix. **read**\n\n2. **cleanup-helper: the comment overstates the timeout-only behaviour.** `gitCleanup` catches every error, not only timeouts. Only timeouts print a warning, and every other failure is silent. The old `gitTry` did the same, so behaviour is unchanged, but the intent line \"best-effort cleanups must not fail on a timeout\" does not mention that non-timeout errors are dropped without a trace. The warning also interpolates `args` unsanitised, while the timeout message uses `plain()`. The args are fixed ref names, so this is harmless. **read**\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n3. **A timed-out push leaves local and remote state out of step.** In `round()` and `syncReviews()`, a `git push` that hits the 600 s limit throws a timedOut `GitError`. The inner catch only handles `fetch first`, so the sync fails before `notes merge -s ours` records what the remote now has. The remote may already have accepted the push. The next sync's merge base is then stale, and the author's own comment says that base must be right. This is not listed among the risks. It is probably self-healing on the next round, but I did not test that. **read**\n\n4. **The `expired()` heuristic in `test/run.ts` can misreport a child.** A child killed by SIGKILL from outside (for example the OOM killer) near the limit is reported as a timeout. This affects tests only and is minor. **read**\n\n5. **The SIGTERM lock-file rationale is stated as fact but only tested with a fake git.** The author lists this as a risk, so it is disclosed. The claim that real git removes `.lock` files on SIGTERM is plausible but unverified here. **read**\n\n**Simplifications**\n\nNone needed. The helper design (`rethrowTimeout`, `gitCleanup`, `NETWORK_TIMEOUT`) is small and matches its tests.\n\n**Accurate claims:** the 120 s default and `GIT_GLOSS_GIT_TIMEOUT` parsing, the 600 s network limit and its env overrides, the distinct timeout message, and the single warning line on a cleanup timeout. Each is backed by a test that passed in my run.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
